# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# black-hole-fall (Miya's Note). Script from miya 2026-11-09; fact-checked per guideline 12-1 (see script-check.md).
SCENES = [
 ('S01', 'The Question', 'ill', 'p01a', 'What would you see, and what would you feel, if you fell into a black hole? Imagine drifting toward a supermassive one in a spacecraft, with a friend watching from far away. One of you will see something very different from the other.'),
 ('S02', 'The Question', 'ill', 'p02a', 'Take a quiet look at the palm of your hand right now. Feel the steady, invisible weight pulling your fingers down toward the floor. You live inside an ocean of gravity every single second, yet you barely notice it.'),
 ('S03', 'The Question', 'ill', 'p02b', 'What if you pushed that familiar pull to its absolute breaking point, and stepped directly into the dark mouth of a black hole?'),
 ('S04', 'Not a Vacuum Cleaner', 'ill', 'p03a', 'Most of us imagine a black hole as a terrifying cosmic vacuum cleaner roaming the universe. But the reality painted by modern physics is far stranger, far quieter, and infinitely more eerie than any monster you could imagine.'),
 ('S05', 'Not a Vacuum Cleaner', 'ill', 'p03b', 'A black hole does not violently chase you across the cosmos. Instead, it simply waits, bending the fabric of space and time into a bottomless slope. If you drifted too close, the universe would not tear you apart with loud explosions.'),
 ('S06', 'Not a Vacuum Cleaner', 'ill', 'p03c', 'It would rewrite your reality, sentence by sentence, until your very future ceased to exist.'),
 ('S07', 'The Fall Begins', 'ill', 'p04a', 'To understand this journey, we have to imagine that you are floating in an advanced spacecraft far outside our solar system. Out here, deep in the cold void, there is no air, no sound, and no light except for the distant pinpricks of ancient stars.'),
 ('S08', 'The Fall Begins', 'ill', 'p04b', 'You are going to steer your ship toward a solitary, non-rotating black hole, step outside into the abyss, and fall directly toward its center. As your engines shut down, an absolute, profound silence settles around your helmet. The dark journey begins with a gentle drift.'),
 ('S09', 'The Shadow in the Stars', 'ill', 'p05a', 'At first, you cannot see the black hole at all because it emits no light of its own. What you see instead is a breathtaking optical illusion playing across the starry background.'),
 ('S10', 'The Shadow in the Stars', 'ill', 'p05b', 'Because this object has packed millions, or even billions, of times the mass of our Sun into a tiny region, its gravity bends the paths of light passing nearby. Distant stars behind the black hole do not appear where they actually belong.'),
 ('S11', 'The Shadow in the Stars', 'ill', 'p05c', 'Their light gets twisted into shimmering arcs, rings, and mirrored duplicates that dance around a growing circle of pure, absolute darkness. It looks like a magnifying glass made of pure emptiness resting on the velvet fabric of the cosmos.'),
 ('S12', 'Gravity and Time', 'ill', 'p06a', 'Now, a strange paradox begins to split your reality in two. Up to this moment, you and your friend watching from the safe mother ship have shared the same clock.'),
 ('S13', 'Gravity and Time', 'ill', 'p06b', 'But Albert Einstein taught us that gravity and time are inextricably bound together. The stronger the gravity you experience, the slower your clock ticks relative to someone standing far away in weaker gravity.'),
 ('S14', 'Gravity and Time', 'ill', 'p06c', 'As you fall deeper into this immense gravitational well, time begins to stretch, creating two completely different versions of the exact same story.'),
 ('S15', 'Your Friend Sees You Slow', 'ill', 'p07a', 'From the perspective of your friend safely watching through a telescope millions of kilometers away, your journey seems to slow down. As you approach the boundary known as the event horizon, they notice your movements becoming strangely sluggish.'),
 ('S16', 'Your Friend Sees You Slow', 'ill', 'p07b', 'Every wave of your hand takes longer and longer to complete. Your ship does not crash into a wall or plunge inward at blinding speed. Instead, to your friend’s eyes, you appear to decelerate as if you were moving through invisible, thickening molasses.'),
 ('S17', 'The Light Turns Red', 'ill', 'p08a', 'Then comes the light. Every beam of light your suit emits must fight against the crushing gravitational pull to escape toward the mother ship. As that light struggles upward, its wavelengths get stretched out, losing energy in a process called gravitational redshift.'),
 ('S18', 'The Light Turns Red', 'ill', 'p08b', 'To your friend, your white spacesuit begins to shift toward a dull orange, then a deep crimson red. The flashes from your beacon grow dimmer and dimmer, arriving later and later at the telescope.'),
 ('S19', 'The Light Turns Red', 'ill', 'p09a', 'Eventually, the wavelength of your light stretches so far that it slips entirely out of the visible spectrum. Your image shifts into infrared, then fades into faint radio waves, until the last detectable photon finally registers on their instruments.'),
 ('S20', 'The Light Turns Red', 'ill', 'p09b', 'To the outside observer, you never actually cross the event horizon. You simply freeze at the threshold of the abyss, growing redder, fainter, and more ghostly, until your image fades into permanent darkness.'),
 ('S21', 'The Light Turns Red', 'ill', 'p09c', 'In their timeline, your crossing would take an infinite amount of time to observe.'),
 ('S22', 'What You Feel', 'ill', 'p10a', 'How can that be true if you are the one falling? What do you actually experience as you look down at the horizon with your own eyes? For you, floating freely in your suit, time does not slow down at all.'),
 ('S23', 'What You Feel', 'ill', 'p10b', 'Your wristwatch ticks with the exact same steady rhythm it had when you were sitting at home on Earth. As far as your personal timeline is concerned, nothing freezes, and you do not wait an eternity. You fall smoothly, accelerating steadily toward the dark veil ahead.'),
 ('S24', 'The Horizon', 'ill', 'p11a', 'As you gaze toward the event horizon, you might expect to see a solid black wall or a painted line marking the boundary of doom. But you see nothing of the sort.'),
 ('S25', 'The Horizon', 'ill', 'p11b', 'The event horizon is not a physical surface made of matter. It is a purely mathematical boundary in space, defined as the precise distance where the escape velocity equals the speed of light.'),
 ('S26', 'The Horizon', 'ill', 'p11c', 'Because you are in free fall, you do not feel any sudden jolt or thud as you glide through it. If you closed your eyes, you would feel completely weightless, just like an astronaut floating in orbit.'),
 ('S27', 'Tidal Forces', 'ill', 'p12a', 'Gravity weakens with distance, which means that any object falling feet-first feels a stronger gravitational pull at its feet than at its head. On Earth, this difference across the height of a human body is so microscopic that our senses cannot detect it.'),
 ('S28', 'Tidal Forces', 'ill', 'p12b', 'But near a small, stellar-mass black hole, the event horizon is tiny, perhaps only thirty kilometers across. Because the center is so extraordinarily close to the surface, the steepness of the gravitational slope across just two meters of space becomes catastrophic.'),
 ('S29', 'The Giant', 'ill', 'p13a', 'Fortunately for you, in our thought experiment, you chose a supermassive black hole, a colossal giant weighing billions of times more than our Sun. These giants sit at the hearts of galaxies, and their event horizons can span billions of kilometers.'),
 ('S30', 'The Giant', 'ill', 'p13b', 'Because the horizon is located so immense a distance from the central mass, the gravitational gradient across your two-meter body remains remarkably gentle. At the horizon of a supermassive black hole, the tidal difference between your head and your toes is virtually imperceptible. You pass through the point of no return completely unharmed.'),
 ('S31', 'Point of No Return', 'ill', 'p14a', 'Yet, the moment your boots cross that mathematical threshold, your relationship with the universe alters permanently and irreversibly. Outside the horizon, you could always fire an imaginary engine with enough power to turn around and fly back toward Earth.'),
 ('S32', 'Point of No Return', 'ill', 'p14b', 'Space is open, and you can travel left, right, forward, backward, or hold your position. But inside the event horizon, space and time effectively swap roles, in a way that defies everyday human intuition.'),
 ('S33', 'The Sky Inside', 'ill', 'p15a', 'What does the sky look like now that you are inside? You might expect everything to go completely black. That is a common misconception. Remember, light from the universe behind you is still falling into the black hole alongside you.'),
 ('S34', 'The Sky Inside', 'ill', 'p15b', 'As you look back over your shoulder toward the cosmos you left behind, you can still see the stars shining. However, their light is no longer spread across an open dome.'),
 ('S35', 'The Sky Inside', 'ill', 'p15c', 'Simulations of such a fall show the outside universe squeezed into a bright circle behind you, shrinking as you fall, while darkness opens ahead.'),
 ('S36', 'The Singularity', 'ill', 'p16a', 'Inside the horizon, the direction toward the center stops being just a direction in space. It becomes the direction of your future, as unavoidable as tomorrow. The singularity is not a place ahead of you.'),
 ('S37', 'The Singularity', 'ill', 'p16b', 'It is a moment in time, an inevitable destination on your personal calendar. In general relativity, it is where time ends for anyone who enters. Classical general relativity predicts that all the mass of the black hole has collapsed down to a point of zero volume and infinite density.'),
 ('S38', 'The Singularity', 'ill', 'p16c', 'At this point, the curvature of spacetime becomes infinitely steep, which is simply a polite way of saying that the laws of physics as we know them break down entirely.'),
 ('S39', 'The Stretch', 'ill', 'p17a', 'As you continue your inevitable descent toward the core of our supermassive black hole, the gentle ride finally comes to a terrifying close. No matter how massive the black hole was at the start, as you draw near the central singularity, the gravitational field lines converge with absolute brutality.'),
 ('S40', 'The Stretch', 'ill', 'p17b', 'The tidal forces that were harmless at the outer horizon now grow exponentially stronger with every millimeter you fall.'),
 ('S41', 'The Stretch', 'ill', 'p18a', 'First, you feel a faint, persistent tension stretching your spine, as if someone were pulling your boots while holding your shoulders. Within fractions of a second, that gentle tug turns into an irresistible, tearing force.'),
 ('S42', 'The Stretch', 'ill', 'p18b', 'The difference in gravitational acceleration between the top of your head and the soles of your feet spikes into thousands of gravities, then millions. Your spacesuit is shredded, the bonds between your molecules are pulled apart, and finally, the very atoms of your body are stretched into long, thin ribbons of matter.'),
 ('S43', 'The Information Paradox', 'ill', 'p19a', 'All of your physical matter reaches the central singularity, merging completely with the mass of the black hole. But here, at the boundary of the infinite, modern science runs headfirst into its greatest unresolved crisis.'),
 ('S44', 'The Information Paradox', 'ill', 'p19b', 'Albert Einstein’s general relativity tells us that the singularity is a point of infinite density, where space and time simply terminate. Yet quantum mechanics, the rulebook governing the subatomic world, insists that fundamental information can never be truly destroyed.'),
 ('S45', 'The Information Paradox', 'ill', 'p19c', 'Most physicists suspect a deeper theory of quantum gravity will replace the infinite density.'),
 ('S46', 'The Information Paradox', 'ill', 'p20a', 'When you fell into the black hole, you carried an enormous amount of complex information with you. You had a specific arrangement of atoms, a unique DNA sequence, and a lifetime of thoughts, memories, and neural patterns.'),
 ('S47', 'The Information Paradox', 'ill', 'p20b', 'If the classical singularity simply crushes all of that into nonexistence, then one of the fundamental pillars of quantum physics is violated. This deep intellectual collision is what physicists call the black hole information paradox, and it has baffled the brightest minds on Earth for decades.'),
 ('S48', 'Hawking Radiation', 'ill', 'p21a', 'In the 1970s, theoretical physicist Stephen Hawking added another layer of mystery to this cosmic puzzle. Using the principles of quantum mechanics near the event horizon, he demonstrated that black holes are not completely black.'),
 ('S49', 'Hawking Radiation', 'ill', 'p21b', 'A popular way to picture it goes like this. In the vacuum of empty space, pairs of virtual particles are constantly popping into existence and annihilating each other almost instantaneously.'),
 ('S50', 'Hawking Radiation', 'ill', 'p21c', 'But right at the edge of the event horizon, a dramatic split can occur.'),
 ('S51', 'Hawking Radiation', 'ill', 'p22a', 'One particle can fall across the horizon into the black hole, while its partner escapes outward into the open universe before they can annihilate. To a distant observer, the black hole appears to emit a faint, steady thermal glow known as Hawking radiation.'),
 ('S52', 'Hawking Radiation', 'ill', 'p22b', 'Because the escaping particle carries away a tiny fraction of energy, the black hole must lose mass to balance the cosmic ledger. If given enough time, an isolated black hole will not last forever. It will slowly, quietly evaporate away into pure heat and light.'),
 ('S53', 'Where Did It Go?', 'ill', 'p23a', 'This opened up a haunting question. If a black hole eventually evaporates into a featureless mist of thermal radiation, what happened to the information of the astronaut who fell inside?'),
 ('S54', 'Where Did It Go?', 'ill', 'p23b', 'Did all your memories, your atoms, and your personal history vanish from the cosmos forever? Or is that information somehow imprinted onto the faint whispers of Hawking radiation, scrambled beyond all recognition but still preserved in the quantum fabric of reality?'),
 ('S55', 'What We Cannot Know', 'ill', 'p24a', 'Yet, as fascinating as these theoretical frameworks are, we must be honest about the boundaries of human knowledge. Because no signal, no radio wave, and no probe can ever escape from beneath the event horizon to report back to our laboratories, we cannot verify what truly happens at the center.'),
 ('S56', 'What We Cannot Know', 'ill', 'p24b', 'The core of a black hole remains the ultimate cosmic vault, guarding its secrets behind a permanent one-way barrier that neither human eyes nor telescopes can ever penetrate.'),
 ('S57', 'Look Up Tonight', 'ill', 'p25a', 'The next time you look up into the clear night sky, gaze past the familiar constellations and into the deep velvet patches of black between the stars. Remember that out there, hidden in the silent dark, time is bending, light is dancing in circles, and the universe is keeping its greatest secrets locked behind horizons of pure shadow.'),
 ('S58', 'Look Up Tonight', 'ill', 'p25b', 'We may never stand on the threshold of an event horizon with our own feet, but with curiosity and reason, human minds have already traveled all the way to the center and back.'),
]
